  {
   "id": "lower6-maths-distributions",
   "title": "Binomial and normal distributions",
   "minutes": 25,
   "programRef": "Cameroon GCE Board — Advanced Level Mathematics, Statistics (to be checked against the official syllabus)",
   "status": "draft",
   "author": "CastBridge (AI draft)",
   "source": "Original writing following the Cameroonian curriculum; to be reviewed by a teacher",
   "objectives": [
    "Use the binomial distribution $B(n,p)$",
    "Standardise a normal variable and use tables",
    "Solve practical problems"
   ],
   "prerequisites": [],
   "exercises": [
    "lower6-maths-statistics-e13",
    "lower6-maths-statistics-e14",
    "lower6-maths-statistics-e15",
    "lower6-maths-statistics-e16",
    "lower6-maths-statistics-e17",
    "lower6-maths-statistics-e18"
   ],
   "selfCheck": [
    "lower6-maths-distributions-q1",
    "lower6-maths-distributions-q2",
    "lower6-maths-distributions-q3",
    "lower6-maths-distributions-q4",
    "lower6-maths-distributions-q5"
   ],
   "reviewNotes": [
    "Table values are computed from the normal CDF and rounded to 4 d.p.; if the Board's tables differ in the last digit, adjust tolerances.",
    "Normal approximation to the binomial and continuity corrections are not included; Poisson is in the Upper Sixth pack.",
    "Syllabus references, scope and level of difficulty are to be checked against the official text; all facts, formulas and worked examples to be verified by a teacher before release."
   ],
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "formule",
     "title": "Binomial $B(n,p)$",
     "md": "Used when there are $n$ independent trials, each with two outcomes and constant success probability $p$. $P(X=r)=C(n,r)p^r(1-p)^{n-r}$. Mean $np$, variance $np(1-p)$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "formule",
     "title": "Normal $N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$",
     "md": "A symmetric bell-shaped curve centred on $\\mu$. Standardise: $Z=\\frac{X-\\mu}{\\sigma}$, which follows $N(0,1)$. Use $\\Phi(z)=P(Z<z)$ from tables. Symmetry: $\\Phi(-z)=1-\\Phi(z)$; $P(Z>z)=1-\\Phi(z)$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "propriete",
     "title": "Table values used here",
     "md": "$\\Phi(1)=0.8413$, $\\Phi(1.5)=0.9332$, $\\Phi(1.96)=0.9750$, $\\Phi(2)=0.9772$. About 68% of a normal distribution lies within $1\\sigma$ of the mean, 95% within $1.96\\sigma$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "- Using the binomial when trials are not independent or $p$ changes.\n- Forgetting to divide by $\\sigma$ (not $\\sigma^2$) when standardising: if $N(55,10^2)$ then $\\sigma=10$.\n- For $P(Z>z)$, subtract the table value from 1."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 1",
     "statement": "$X\\sim B(10,0.3)$. Find $P(X=3)$.",
     "steps": [
      "$P=C(10,3)(0.3)^3(0.7)^7$ with $C(10,3)=120$.",
      "$=120\\times0.027\\times0.08235=0.2668$."
     ],
     "answer": "$P(X=3)\\approx0.267$"
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 2",
     "statement": "Heights of adults in a village are $N(170,8^2)$ cm. Find $P(X>182)$.",
     "steps": [
      "$z=\\frac{182-170}{8}=1.5$.",
      "$P(Z>1.5)=1-\\Phi(1.5)=1-0.9332$."
     ],
     "answer": "$0.0668$",
     "figure": {
      "kind": "plot",
      "xmin": -3.5,
      "xmax": 3.5,
